<mujoco model="conveyor section (adapted from MuJoCo showcase carousel)">
<!--
  Conveyor belt section, adapted from the official MuJoCo showcase model
  "Luggage carousel":  model/surfacevel/carousel.xml
  Source : https://github.com/google-deepmind/mujoco (Apache-2.0)
  Vendored: assets/_upstream_mujoco_carousel.xml (untouched copy for comparison)
  Feature: geom/surfacevel, added upstream in commit 4787c80 (MuJoCo 3.9+).
           A static belt with a surface velocity is transported by friction, so
           the belt needs no joints, no actuators and no teleporting slats.

  The five geoms below keep the upstream structure and the upstream velocity
  relationship exactly:
      belt   : box, surfacevel = (v, 0, 0)
      rail x2: box, side guides standing proud of the belt surface
      roller x2 : cylinder about +Y, surfacevel angular = -(v / r)
  Upstream: v = 0.7 m/s, r = 0.06 m  ->  -11.667 rad/s.
  Here:    v = 0.10 m/s, r = 0.025 m ->  -4.0 rad/s.  That is a normal
           indexing / pick-and-place line speed: the part has to stay inside
           the arm's 160 mm grasp window for the whole pick cycle.

  Changes, all forced by the SO-101's size (a 0.35 m reach arm):
    * belt  0.60 m wide -> 0.096 m wide.  A 600 mm belt reaches 0.3 m to either
      side of its centre line, so at the only reachable lane offset for this
      arm (0.245 m) the belt would sit on top of the robot's own table.
    * rails moved from y = +/-0.33 to y = +/-0.07 so the clear lane is 140 mm.
      That number is set by the gripper, not by taste: the jaw tips sit 27.7 mm
      either side of the grasp point and the finger pads are 15 mm half-width,
      so a lane narrower than about 120 mm stops the arm dead on its way down,
      and a part more than 20 mm off the centre line cannot be reached without
      a finger hitting a rail.
    * the rails are in two segments with an opening at the pick station
      (x = -0.02 .. +0.22), exactly like a real conveyor.  The arm can only be
      square to the belt below z = 0.68 m, and with a continuous rail whose top
      is at 0.685 m there is no height at which the wrist can rotate out of a
      transit pose into a top-down grasp without a finger sweeping into it.
    * roller r 0.06 -> 0.025 so the roller crown stays flush with the belt
      surface (as upstream), and 5 mm short of the rails.
    * belt ends tangent to the rollers instead of inside them: a part rides to
      the crown of the head roller and drops off it, which is how a real
      conveyor discharges.
    * rail top 0.08 m above the belt surface -> 0.06 m, so a 50 mm part is
      guided without hiding it from the camera.
    * belt speed 0.7 -> 0.10 m/s, a normal indexing line speed.
    * LENGTHENED at the TAIL ONLY, by 200 mm: the tail roller moved from
      x = -0.74 to x = -0.94, the belt from 1.43 m to 1.63 m long.  The head
      end is untouched, so the discharge point, the bin, the drive and the
      whole verified pick window keep their measured coordinates and only the
      infeed run got longer.  The tail is where a longer line is free: the arm
      cannot reach x < -0.2 anyway, so nothing about the pick cycle changes.
      Measured after the change: the belt spans x -0.915 .. +0.715, i.e. 1.63 m
      between the roller crowns, 1.68 m between the roller centres.
-->
  <asset>
    <material name="belt" rgba="0.20 0.22 0.26 1"/>
    <material name="rail" rgba="0.62 0.64 0.67 1"/>
  </asset>

  <worldbody>
    <frame name="conveyor">
      <geom name="belt" type="box" size="0.815 0.048 0.025" pos="-0.10 0 0"
            material="belt" surfacevel="0.10 0 0  0 0 0"
            friction="1.0 0.005 0.0001" priority="1"/>
      <geom name="rail_tail" type="box" size="0.46 0.005 0.09" pos="-0.48 -0.07 -0.005"
            material="rail" friction="0.4 0.005 0.0001"/>
      <geom name="rail_head" type="box" size="0.26 0.005 0.09" pos="0.48 0.07 -0.005"
            material="rail" friction="0.4 0.005 0.0001"/>
      <geom name="rail_tail_far" type="box" size="0.46 0.005 0.09" pos="-0.48 0.07 -0.005"
            material="rail" friction="0.4 0.005 0.0001"/>
      <geom name="rail_head_far" type="box" size="0.26 0.005 0.09" pos="0.48 -0.07 -0.005"
            material="rail" friction="0.4 0.005 0.0001"/>
      <geom name="roller_tail" type="cylinder" size="0.025 0.045" pos="-0.94 0 0"
            euler="90 0 0" material="rail" surfacevel="0 0 0  0 0 -4"/>
      <geom name="roller_head" type="cylinder" size="0.025 0.045" pos="0.74 0 0"
            euler="90 0 0" material="rail" surfacevel="0 0 0  0 0 -4"/>
    </frame>
  </worldbody>
</mujoco>



